[{"input":"function test() {\n  return 42;\n}","output":"","result":"","resultHTML":"","hidden":false,"type":"code"},{"input":"# Black holes\n\nA **black hole** is a region of spacetime whose gravity is so strong that no future-directed signal from inside its **event horizon** can reach distant observers. In general relativity, gravity is not a force acting across a fixed background: mass, energy and momentum curve spacetime, and objects and light follow paths determined by that geometry. The horizon is a causal boundary, not a material surface.\n\nFor an ideal, non-rotating, uncharged black hole of mass ``M``, the Schwarzschild radius is\n\n```math\nr_s = \\frac{2GM}{c^2}\n```\n\nwhere ``G`` is the gravitational constant and ``c`` is the speed of light. For one solar mass, ``r_s`` is about 2.95 km. The same expression arises from setting Newtonian escape speed equal to ``c``, but the causal meaning of the horizon requires general relativity.\n\nA black hole formed by a collapsing star is expected to rotate; an isolated, uncharged rotating black hole is described by the **Kerr solution**. Rotation changes the horizon geometry and creates an **ergosphere**, where spacetime is dragged around so strongly that observers cannot remain stationary relative to distant stars. Energy can be extracted from rotation through processes involving this region.\n\nAn observer falling through the horizon of a sufficiently massive black hole need not encounter a locally special surface. Nevertheless, once inside, all future-directed paths lead toward smaller radii. Classical general relativity predicts a singularity, where its description breaks down; a complete quantum theory of gravity is expected to be needed there. Tidal forces—the differences in gravitational acceleration across an object—can stretch and compress matter, and are generally stronger at the horizon of a less massive black hole.\n\nBlack holes are detected indirectly: hot accreting gas emits radiation; stars and gas orbit an unseen compact mass; and mergers produce gravitational waves. Images of the bright emission around supermassive black holes reveal a central **shadow**, which is larger than the horizon and depends on light bending and the surrounding plasma. Quantum field theory also predicts **Hawking radiation**: a black hole has a temperature inversely proportional to its mass and can slowly lose mass, though this effect is negligible for astrophysical black holes on observational timescales.","output":"","result":"# Black holes\n\nA **black hole** is a region of spacetime whose gravity is so strong that no future-directed signal from inside its **event horizon** can reach distant observers. In general relativity, gravity is not a force acting across a fixed background: mass, energy and momentum curve spacetime, and objects and light follow paths determined by that geometry. The horizon is a causal boundary, not a material surface.\n\nFor an ideal, non-rotating, uncharged black hole of mass ``M``, the Schwarzschild radius is\n\n```math\nr_s = \\frac{2GM}{c^2}\n```\n\nwhere ``G`` is the gravitational constant and ``c`` is the speed of light. For one solar mass, ``r_s`` is about 2.95 km. The same expression arises from setting Newtonian escape speed equal to ``c``, but the causal meaning of the horizon requires general relativity.\n\nA black hole formed by a collapsing star is expected to rotate; an isolated, uncharged rotating black hole is described by the **Kerr solution**. Rotation changes the horizon geometry and creates an **ergosphere**, where spacetime is dragged around so strongly that observers cannot remain stationary relative to distant stars. Energy can be extracted from rotation through processes involving this region.\n\nAn observer falling through the horizon of a sufficiently massive black hole need not encounter a locally special surface. Nevertheless, once inside, all future-directed paths lead toward smaller radii. Classical general relativity predicts a singularity, where its description breaks down; a complete quantum theory of gravity is expected to be needed there. Tidal forces—the differences in gravitational acceleration across an object—can stretch and compress matter, and are generally stronger at the horizon of a less massive black hole.\n\nBlack holes are detected indirectly: hot accreting gas emits radiation; stars and gas orbit an unseen compact mass; and mergers produce gravitational waves. Images of the bright emission around supermassive black holes reveal a central **shadow**, which is larger than the horizon and depends on light bending and the surrounding plasma. Quantum field theory also predicts **Hawking radiation**: a black hole has a temperature inversely proportional to its mass and can slowly lose mass, though this effect is negligible for astrophysical black holes on observational timescales.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Untitled-c3916-black-holes\">\n<h1>Black holes</h1>\n<p>A <strong>black hole</strong> is a region of spacetime whose gravity is so strong that no future-directed signal from inside its <strong>event horizon</strong> can reach distant observers. In general relativity, gravity is not a force acting across a fixed background: mass, energy and momentum curve spacetime, and objects and light follow paths determined by that geometry. The horizon is a causal boundary, not a material surface.</p>\n<p>For an ideal, non-rotating, uncharged black hole of mass <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M\\)</span>, the Schwarzschild radius is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nr_s = \\frac{2GM}{c^2}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(G\\)</span> is the gravitational constant and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span> is the speed of light. For one solar mass, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r_s\\)</span> is about 2.95 km. The same expression arises from setting Newtonian escape speed equal to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(c\\)</span>, but the causal meaning of the horizon requires general relativity.</p>\n<p>A black hole formed by a collapsing star is expected to rotate; an isolated, uncharged rotating black hole is described by the <strong>Kerr solution</strong>. Rotation changes the horizon geometry and creates an <strong>ergosphere</strong>, where spacetime is dragged around so strongly that observers cannot remain stationary relative to distant stars. Energy can be extracted from rotation through processes involving this region.</p>\n<p>An observer falling through the horizon of a sufficiently massive black hole need not encounter a locally special surface. Nevertheless, once inside, all future-directed paths lead toward smaller radii. Classical general relativity predicts a singularity, where its description breaks down; a complete quantum theory of gravity is expected to be needed there. Tidal forces—the differences in gravitational acceleration across an object—can stretch and compress matter, and are generally stronger at the horizon of a less massive black hole.</p>\n<p>Black holes are detected indirectly: hot accreting gas emits radiation; stars and gas orbit an unseen compact mass; and mergers produce gravitational waves. Images of the bright emission around supermassive black holes reveal a central <strong>shadow</strong>, which is larger than the horizon and depends on light bending and the surrounding plasma. Quantum field theory also predicts <strong>Hawking radiation</strong>: a black hole has a temperature inversely proportional to its mass and can slowly lose mass, though this effect is negligible for astrophysical black holes on observational timescales.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## An image of a black hole\n\n![A bright, asymmetric ring of emission around the dark shadow of the black hole M87*](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg)\n\nThe supermassive black hole M87\\*, imaged by the Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration and released in 2019.[^image-credit] The bright ring is radiation from surrounding hot gas; the dark central region is the black hole's shadow, not a direct photograph of the event horizon.\n\n[^image-credit]:\n\n    Image credit: Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration. [Image source](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg).","output":"","result":"## An image of a black hole\n\n![A bright, asymmetric ring of emission around the dark shadow of the black hole M87*](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg)\n\nThe supermassive black hole M87\\*, imaged by the Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration and released in 2019.[^image-credit] The bright ring is radiation from surrounding hot gas; the dark central region is the black hole's shadow, not a direct photograph of the event horizon.\n\n[^image-credit]:\n\n    Image credit: Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration. [Image source](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg).","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Untitled-c3917-an-image-of-a-black-hole\">\n<h1>An image of a black hole</h1>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg\" alt=\"A bright, asymmetric ring of emission around the dark shadow of the black hole M87*\">\n</figure>\n<p>The supermassive black hole M87*, imaged by the Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration and released in 2019.<a href=\"#nb-Notebooks_Untitled-c3917-image-credit\" data-type=\"footnote\"></a> The bright ring is radiation from surrounding hot gas; the dark central region is the black hole’s shadow, not a direct photograph of the event horizon.</p>\n<div data-type=\"footnote\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Untitled-c3917-image-credit\">\n<p>Image credit: Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration. <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg\">Image source</a>.</p>\n</div>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]